NATIVE REVERSE-ENGINEERING DUMP
================================
Archival export of confirmed native binary reverse-engineering findings.
All findings were established through read-only live memory analysis of the
running csgo.exe process plus static analysis of the mapped module. No binary
or source file was modified during this investigation. No new investigation
was performed for this document.

========================================
1. BINARY IDENTIFICATION
========================================

Module concept: The Neverlose cheat core is NOT the on-disk neverlose.dll.
On-disk neverlose.dll (56,370,688 bytes, SHA256
529C59BD687901D4E86898F78F50534CA4FC5611D693C296C9A3BD7BF8F61253) is a
loader stub: pattern scans against it yield zero matches for every cheat
signature. The real cheat code is manually mapped into the csgo.exe process
address space.

Architecture: x86 32-bit (little-endian). The csgo.exe process itself is
32-bit; Get-Process.Modules reports only the WOW64 64-bit view, so all
addresses below are 32-bit virtual addresses inside the manually mapped
allocation.

Allocation base: 0x412A0000
Allocation size:   0x00DBA000 (3,410,614 bytes)
Protection:        PAGE_EXECUTE_WRITECOPY (WCX)
Anchor instruction: F3 0F 5B CA 0F 5B C9 0F 2E CB at 0x41400D8F
   Used by get_base_address() in MUST UPDATE.txt to locate the region;
   region.AllocationBase is returned as the module base.
Scan delta:        0x340AB6 = furthest_addr 0x4158F57C - closest_addr 0x4124EAC6
   Pattern scan_nl operates over [base, base + delta). This delta was
   embedded in MUST UPDATE.txt memory.main.scan_nl and is consistent with
   the mapped image extent.
Continuation region: 0x4205A000, size 0x001DF000, protection R (read-only,
   page-guard / NOACCESS guard pages adjacent).
Small WCX thunk pages interspersed around the main allocation.
Observed process: csgo.exe, confirmed running. PID was 8780 during the
   earlier Phase 3 audit (working set ~1059 MB) and 21580 during this
   Phase 5 investigation (working set ~1067 MB). All addresses below are
   valid for PID 21580; they are module-relative so they are stable across
